(function(){if(!window.__hermes_register_book)return;window.__hermes_register_book("sappho-fragments",[{"sp":"HDR","t":"The Poems and Fragments"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"I. In Sapphic Metre","st":"1"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Immortal Aphrodite of the broidered throne, daughter of Zeus, weaver of wiles, I pray thee break not my spirit with anguish and distress, O Queen. But come hither, if ever before thou didst hear my voice afar, and listen, and leaving thy father's golden house camest with chariot yoked, and fair fleet sparrows drew thee, flapping fast their wings around the dark earth, from heaven through mid sky. Quickly arrived they; and thou, blessed one, smiling with immortal countenance, didst ask What now is befallen me, and Why now I call, and What I in my mad heart most desire to see. 'What Beauty now wouldst thou draw to love thee? Who wrongs thee, Sappho? For even if she flies she shall soon follow, and if she rejects gifts shall yet give, and if she loves not shall soon love, however loth.' Come, I pray thee, now too, and release me from cruel cares; and all that my heart desires to accomplish, accomplish thou, and be thyself my ally.<\/div>","st":"1"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Dionysius of Halicarnassus, writing at Rome about 25 B.C., quotes this, commonly called <em>The Ode to Aphrodite<\/em>, as a perfect illustration of the elaborately finished style of poetry, showing in detail how its grace and beauty lie in the subtle harmony between the words and the ideas. Certain lines of it, though nowhere else the whole, are preserved by Hephaestion and other authors.<\/div>","st":"1"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">That man seems to me peer of gods, who sits in thy presence, and hears close to him thy sweet speech and lovely laughter; that indeed makes my heart flutter in my bosom. For when I see thee but a little, I have no utterance left, my tongue is broken down, and straightway a subtle fire has run under my skin, with my eyes I have no sight, my ears ring, sweat pours down, and a trembling seizes all my body; I am paler than grass, and seem in my madness little better than one dead. But I must dare all, since one so poor ...<\/div>","st":"2"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Longinus, about 250 A.D., uses this, <em>The Ode to Anactoria<\/em>, or <em>To a beloved Woman<\/em>, or <em>To a Maiden<\/em>, as tradition variously names it, to illustrate the perfection of the Sublime in poetry, calling it 'not one passion, but a congress of passions,' and showing how Sappho had here seized upon the signs of love-frenzy and harmonised them into faultless phrase. Plutarch had, about 60 A.D., spoken of this ode as 'mixed with fire,' and quoted Philoxenus as referring to Sappho's 'sweet-voiced songs healing love.'<\/div>","st":"2"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">The stars about the fair moon in their turn hide their bright face when she at about her full lights up all earth with silver.<\/div>","st":"3"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Eustathius of Thessalonica, late in the twelfth century, to illustrate the simile in the <em>Iliad<\/em>, viii. 551. Julian, about 350 A.D., says Sappho applied the epithet <em>silver<\/em> to the moon; wherefore Blomfield suggested its position here.<\/div>","st":"3"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">And round about the [breeze] murmurs cool through apple-boughs, and slumber streams from quivering leaves.<\/div>","st":"4"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Cited by Hermogenes, about 170 A.D., as an example of simple style, and to show the pleasure given by description. The fragment describes the gardens of the nymphs, which Demetrius, about 150 A.D., says were sung by Sappho.<\/div>","st":"4"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Come, goddess of Cyprus, and in golden cups serve nectar delicately mixed with delights.<\/div>","st":"5"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus, a native of Naucratis, who flourished about 230 A.D., quotes these verses as an example of the poets' custom of invoking Aphrodite in their pledges.<\/div>","st":"5"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Or Cyprus and Paphos, or Panormus [holds] thee.<\/div>","st":"6"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Strabo, about 19 A.D.<\/div>","st":"6"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">But for thee will I [lead] to the altar [the offspring] of a white goat ... and add a libation for thee.<\/div>","st":"7-8"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Adduced by Apollonius of Alexandria, about 140 A.D., to illustrate similarities in dialects.<\/div>","st":"7-8"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">This lot may I win, golden-crowned Aphrodite.<\/div>","st":"9"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Apollonius, to show how adverbs give an idea of prayer.<\/div>","st":"9"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Who gave me their gifts and made me honoured.<\/div>","st":"10"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Apollonius, to illustrate the Aeolic dialect.<\/div>","st":"10"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">This will I now sing deftly to please my girl-friends.<\/div>","st":"11"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Athenaeus to prove that freeborn women and maidens often called their girl associates and friends ἐταῖραι (<em>Hetaerae<\/em>), without any idea of reproach.<\/div>","st":"11"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">For they whom I benefit injure me most.<\/div>","st":"12"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From the <em>Etymologicum Magnum<\/em>, a dictionary which was compiled about the tenth century A.D.<\/div>","st":"12"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">But that which one desires I ...<\/div>","st":"13"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Apollonius, to illustrate the use of the verb ἐράω.<\/div>","st":"13"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">To you, fair maids, my mind changes not.<\/div>","st":"14"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Apollonius, to show the Aeolic use of ὔμμιν for ὑμῖν, 'to you.'<\/div>","st":"14"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">And this I feel in myself.<\/div>","st":"15"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Apollonius, to show Aeolic accentuation.<\/div>","st":"15"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">But their heart turned cold and they dropt their wings.<\/div>","st":"16"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">In Pindar, <em>Pyth.<\/em> i. 10, the eagle of Zeus, delighted by music, drops his wings, and the Scholiast quotes this fragment to show that Sappho says the same of doves.<\/div>","st":"16"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">According to my weeping: it and all care let buffeting winds bear away.<\/div>","st":"17"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From the <em>Etymologicum Magnum<\/em>, to show that the Aeolians used ζ in the place of σσ. Ἄμοι is a guess of Bergk's for ἄνεμοι, 'winds.'<\/div>","st":"17"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Me just now the golden-sandalled Dawn ...<\/div>","st":"18"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Ammonius of Alexandria, at the close of the fourth century A.D., to show Sappho's use of ἀρτίως.<\/div>","st":"18"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">A broidered strap of fair Lydian work covered her feet.<\/div>","st":"19"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by the Scholiast on Aristophanes' <em>Peace<\/em>, 1174; and also by Pollux, about 180 A.D.<\/div>","st":"19"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Shot with a thousand hues.<\/div>","st":"20"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by the Scholiast on Apollonius of Rhodes, i. 727, in speaking of Jason's double-folded mantle having been reddish instead of flame-coloured.<\/div>","st":"20"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Me thou forgettest.<\/div>","st":"21"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Apollonius, as is also the following, to show the Aeolic use of ἔμεθεν for ἐμοῦ, 'of me.'<\/div>","st":"21"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Or lovest another more than me.<\/div>","st":"22"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Ye are nought to me.<\/div>","st":"23"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Apollonius, as is also the following fragment, to show that ὑμεῖς was in Aeolic ὔμμες 'you.'<\/div>","st":"23"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">While ye will.<\/div>","st":"24"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">I yearn and seek ...<\/div>","st":"25"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From the <em>Etymologicum Magnum<\/em>, to show that the Aeolians used ποθήω for ποθέω, 'I yearn.'<\/div>","st":"25"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">O Muse of the golden throne, raise that strain which the reverend elder of Teos, from the goodly land of fair women, used to sing so sweetly.<\/div>","st":"26"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus says 'Hermesianax was mistaken when he represented Sappho and Anacreon as contemporaries, for Anacreon lived in the time of Cyrus and Polycrates [probably 563-478 B.C.], but Sappho lived in the reign of Alyattes the father of Croesus. But Chamaeleon, in his treatise on Sappho, asserts that according to some these verses were made upon her by Anacreon:— ...' Then follows Sappho's reply, the present fragment. Probably the whole is spurious, for certainly Sappho never saw Anacreon: she must have died before he was born. Even Athenaeus says that it is clear to every one that the verses are not Sappho's.<\/div>","st":"26"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"II. In Dactylic Metre","st":"27"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">When anger spreads through the breast, guard thy tongue from barking idly.<\/div>","st":"27"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Plutarch, in his treatise <em>On restraining anger<\/em>, to show that in wrath nothing is more noble than quietness.<\/div>","st":"27"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"III. In Alcaic Metre","st":"28"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Hadst thou felt desire for things good or noble, and had not thy tongue framed some evil speech, shame had not filled thine eyes, but thou hadst spoken honestly about it.<\/div>","st":"28"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Aristotle, in his <em>Rhetoric<\/em>, i. 9, about 330 B.C., says 'base things dishonour those who do or wish them, as Sappho showed when Alcaeus said— \"<em>Violet-weaving, pure, softly-smiling Sappho, I would say something, but shame restrains me<\/em>\"', and she answered him in the words of the present fragment.<\/div>","st":"28"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"IV. In Mixed Glyconic and Alcaic Metre","st":"29"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Stand face to face, friend ... and unveil the grace in thine eyes.<\/div>","st":"29"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus, speaking of the charm of lovers' eyes, says Sappho addressed this to a man who was admired above all others for his beauty.<\/div>","st":"29"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"V. In Choriambic Metre","st":"30"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">And golden pulse grew on the shores.<\/div>","st":"30"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Athenaeus, when he is speaking of vetches.<\/div>","st":"30"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Leto and Niobe were friends full dear.<\/div>","st":"31"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Athenaeus for the same reason as fr. 11.<\/div>","st":"31"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Men I think will remember us even hereafter.<\/div>","st":"32"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Dio Chrysostom, the celebrated Greek rhetorician, writing about 100 A.D., observes that Sappho says this 'with perfect beauty.'<\/div>","st":"32"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">I loved thee once, Atthis, long ago.<\/div>","st":"33"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Hephaestion, about 150 A.D., as an example of metre. The verse stood at the beginning of the first ode of the second book of Sappho's poems, which Hephaestion says was composed entirely of odes in this metre.<\/div>","st":"33"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">A slight and ill-favoured child didst thou seem to me.<\/div>","st":"34"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Plutarch; and by others also.<\/div>","st":"34"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Foolish woman, pride not thyself on a ring.<\/div>","st":"35"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Preserved by Herodian the grammarian, who lived about 160 A.D.<\/div>","st":"35"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">I know not what to do; my mind is divided.<\/div>","st":"36"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by the Stoic philosopher Chrysippus, about 220 B.C.<\/div>","st":"36"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">I do not think to touch the sky with my two arms.<\/div>","st":"37"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Herodian.<\/div>","st":"37"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">And I flutter like a child after her mother.<\/div>","st":"38"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted in the <em>Etymologicum Magnum<\/em> as an example of Aeolic.<\/div>","st":"38"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Spring's messenger, the sweet-voiced nightingale.<\/div>","st":"39"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by the Scholiast on Sophocles, <em>Electra<\/em>, 149, 'the nightingale is the messenger of Zeus, because it is the sign of Spring.'<\/div>","st":"39"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Now Love masters my limbs and shakes me, fatal creature, bitter-sweet.<\/div>","st":"40"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Hephaestion.<\/div>","st":"40"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">But to thee, Atthis, the thought of me is hateful; thou flittest to Andromeda.<\/div>","st":"41"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Hephaestion together with fr. 40, but it seems to be the beginning of a different ode.<\/div>","st":"41"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Now Eros shakes my soul, a wind on the mountain falling on the oaks.<\/div>","st":"42"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Maximus Tyrius, about 150 B.C., in speaking of Socrates exciting Phaedrus to Bacchic frenzy when he talked of love.<\/div>","st":"42"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">When all night long [sleep] holds their [eyes].<\/div>","st":"43"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Apollonius to show the Aeolic form of σφί. Bergk thinks that Sappho may have written— ὄππατ' [ἄωρος,] ὄτα πάννυχος ἄσφι κατάγρει, therefore I translate it so.<\/div>","st":"43"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">And purple napkins for thy lap ... (even these wilt thou despise) I sent from Phocaea, precious gifts for thy lap.<\/div>","st":"44"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Athenaeus out of the fifth book of Sappho's <em>Songs to Aphrodite<\/em>, to show that χειρόμακτρα were cloths, handkerchiefs, for covering the head. But the whole passage is hopelessly corrupt.<\/div>","st":"44"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Come now, divine shell, become vocal for me.<\/div>","st":"45"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Hermogenes and Eustathius, of Sappho apostrophising her lyre.<\/div>","st":"45"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">And tender woven garlands round tender neck<\/div>","st":"46"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Athenaeus.<\/div>","st":"46"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Fonder of maids than Gello.<\/div>","st":"47"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted as a proverb by Zenobius, about 130 A.D.; said of those who die an untimely death, or of those whose indulgence brings ruin on their children. Gello was a maiden who died in youth, whose ghost, the Lesbians said, pursued children and carried them off.<\/div>","st":"47"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Of Gorgo full weary.<\/div>","st":"48"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Choeroboscus, about the end of the sixth century A.D., to show that the Aeolic genitive ended in -ως.<\/div>","st":"48"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Of a proud (or perfumed, or flowery) palace.<\/div>","st":"49"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus says Sappho here mentions the 'royal' and the 'brentheian' unguent together, as if they were one and the same thing; but the reading is very uncertain.<\/div>","st":"49"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">But I upon a soft cushion dispose my limbs.<\/div>","st":"50"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Herodian.<\/div>","st":"50"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">And there the bowl of ambrosia was mixed, and Hermes took the ladle to pour out for the gods; and then they all held goblets, and made libation, and wished the bridegroom all good luck.<\/div>","st":"51"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">The first two lines are quoted by Athenaeus to show that in Sappho Hermes was cupbearer to the gods; and in another place he quotes the rest to illustrate her mention of <em>carchēsia,<\/em> cups narrow in the middle, with handles reaching from the top to the bottom. Lachmann first joined the two fragments.<\/div>","st":"51"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">The moon has set, and the Pleiades; it is midnight, the time is going by, and I sleep alone.<\/div>","st":"52"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Hephaestion as an example of metre.<\/div>","st":"52"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">The moon rose full, and the women stood as though around an altar.<\/div>","st":"53"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Hephaestion as an example of Praxilleian verses, <em>i.e.<\/em> such as the Sicyonian poetess Praxilla (about B.C. 450) wrote in the metre known as the Ionic a majore trimeter brachycatalectic.<\/div>","st":"53"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Thus at times with tender feet the Cretan women dance in measure round the fair altar, trampling the fine soft bloom of the grass.<\/div>","st":"54"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Hephaestion as an example of metre, vv. 1 and 2 in one place and v. 3 in another; Bergk says Santen first joined them.<\/div>","st":"54"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Then delicately in thick robe I sprang.<\/div>","st":"55"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Herodian, as an illustration of the Aeolic dialect. Bergk attributes this to Sappho, but Cramer and others think that Alcaeus wrote the line.<\/div>","st":"55"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Leda they say once found an egg hidden under hyacinth-blossoms.<\/div>","st":"56"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From the <em>Etymologicum Magnum<\/em>, Athenaeus, and others.<\/div>","st":"56"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">And dark-eyed Sleep, child of Night.<\/div>","st":"57"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From the <em>Etymologicum Magnum<\/em>, to show that the first letter of ἄωρος = ὦρος, 'sleep,' was redundant.<\/div>","st":"57"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Aphrodite's handmaid bright as gold.<\/div>","st":"57A"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Philodemus, about 60 B.C., in a MS. discovered at Herculaneum, says that Sappho thus addresses Πειθώ, <em>Persuasion<\/em>. The MS. is, however, defective, and Gomperz, the editor, thinks from the context that Hecate is here referred to. (Bergk formerly numbered this fr. 141.)<\/div>","st":"57A"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Andromeda has a fair requital.<\/div>","st":"58"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Hephaestion together with the following, although the lines are obviously out of different odes. Probably each fragment is the first line of separate poems.<\/div>","st":"58"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Sappho, why [celebrate] blissful Aphrodite?<\/div>","st":"59"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Come now, delicate Graces and fair-haired Muses.<\/div>","st":"60"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Hephaestion, Attilius Fortunatianus (about the fifth century A.D.), and Servius, as an example of Sappho's choriambic tetrameters.<\/div>","st":"60"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">A sweet-voiced maiden.<\/div>","st":"61"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Attilius Fortunatianus.<\/div>","st":"61"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Delicate Adonis is dying, Cytherea; what shall we do? Beat your breasts, maidens, and rend your tunics.<\/div>","st":"62"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Hephaestion, and presumed to be Sappho's from a passage in Pausanias, where he says she learnt the name of the mythological personage Oetolĭnus (as if οἶτος Λίνου, 'the death of Linus'), from the poems of Pamphōs, a mythical poet of Attica earlier than Homer, and so to her Adonis was just like Oetolinus.<\/div>","st":"62"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Ah for Adonis!<\/div>","st":"63"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Marius Plotius, about 600 A.D. It seems to be the refrain of the ode to Adonis.<\/div>","st":"63"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Coming from heaven wearing a purple mantle.<\/div>","st":"64"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Pollux, about 180 A.D., who says that Sappho, in her ode to Eros, out of which this verse probably came, was the first to use the word χλαμύς, a short mantle fastened by a brooch on the right shoulder, so as to hang in a curve across the body.<\/div>","st":"64"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Come, rosy-armed pure Graces, daughters of Zeus.<\/div>","st":"65"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Theocritus' <em>Idyl 28<\/em>, <em>On a Distaff<\/em>, according to the argument prefixed to it, was written in the dialect and metre of this fragment. And Philostrătus, about 220 A.D., says 'Sappho loves the rose, and always crowns it with some praise, likening to it the beauty of her maidens; she likens it also to the arms of the Graces, when she describes their elbows bare.'<\/div>","st":"65"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">But Ares says he would drag Hephaestus by force.<\/div>","st":"66"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Priscian, late in the fifth century A.D.<\/div>","st":"66"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Many thousand cups thou drainest.<\/div>","st":"67"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Athenaeus when descanting on drinking-cups.<\/div>","st":"67"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">But thou shalt ever lie dead, nor shall there be any remembrance of thee then or thereafter, for thou hast not of the roses of Pieria; but thou shalt wander obscure even in the house of Hades, flitting among the shadowy dead.<\/div>","st":"68"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Stobaeus, about 500 A.D., as addressed to an uneducated woman. Plutarch quotes the fragment as written to a certain rich lady; but in another work he says the crown of roses was assigned to the Muses, for he remembered Sappho's having said to some unpolished and uneducated woman these same words.<\/div>","st":"68"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">No one maiden I think shall at any time see the sunlight that shall be as wise as thou.<\/div>","st":"69"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Chrysippus. It is probably out of the same ode as the preceding.<\/div>","st":"69"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">What country girl bewitches thy heart, who knows not how to draw her dress about her ankles?<\/div>","st":"70"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus, speaking of the care which the ancients bestowed upon dress, says Sappho thus jests upon Andromeda. Three other authors quote the same lines.<\/div>","st":"70"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">I taught Hero of Gyara, the swift runner.<\/div>","st":"71"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Choeroboscus, to show the Aeolic accusative.<\/div>","st":"71"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">I am not of a malignant nature, but have a quiet temper.<\/div>","st":"72"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted in the <em>Etymologicum Magnum<\/em> to show the meaning of ἀβάκης, 'childlike, innocent.'<\/div>","st":"72"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">But charming [maidens] plaited garlands.<\/div>","st":"73"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by the Scholiast on Aristophanes <em>Thesmophoriazusae<\/em> 401, to show that plaiting wreaths was a sign of being in love.<\/div>","st":"73"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Thou and my servant Love.<\/div>","st":"74"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Maximus Tyrius to show that Sappho agreed with Diotima when the latter said to Socrates (Plato, <em>Sympos.<\/em>, p. 328) that Love is not the son, but the attendant and servant, of Aphrodite.<\/div>","st":"74"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">But if thou lovest us, choose another and a younger bed-fellow; for I will not brook to live with thee, old woman with young man.<\/div>","st":"75"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Stobaeus' <em>Anthology<\/em>, and Apostolius.<\/div>","st":"75"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Mnasidica is more shapely than the tender Gyrinno.<\/div>","st":"76"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Hephaestion as an example of metre.<\/div>","st":"76"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Scornfuler than thee, Eranna, have I nowhere found.<\/div>","st":"77"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Hephaestion with the foregoing. The MSS. do not agree; perhaps ὦ ῎ραννα is an adjective, for ὢ ἐρατεινή, <em>O lovely—<\/em>.<\/div>","st":"77"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Do thou, Dica, set garlands round thy lovely hair, twining shoots of dill together with soft hands: for those who have fair flowers may best stand first, even in the favour of Goddesses; who turn their face away from those who lack garlands.<\/div>","st":"78"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus, quoting this fragment, says:—'Sappho gives a more simple reason for our wearing garlands, speaking as follows ... in which lines she enjoins all who offer sacrifice to wear garlands on their heads, as they are beautiful things and acceptable to the Gods.'<\/div>","st":"78"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">I love delicacy, and for me Love has the sun's splendour and beauty.<\/div>","st":"79"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">In speaking of perfumes, Athenaeus, quoting Clearchus, says:—'Sappho, being a thorough woman and a poetess besides, was ashamed to separate honour from elegance, and speaks thus ... making it evident to everybody that the desire of life that she confessed had brilliancy and honour in it; and these things especially belong to virtue.'<\/div>","st":"79"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">And down I set the cushion.<\/div>","st":"80"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Herodian, along with fr. 50.<\/div>","st":"80"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Wealth without thee, Worth, is no safe neighbour [but the mixture of both is the height of happiness].<\/div>","st":"81"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From the Scholiast on Pindar. The second line appears to be the gloss of the commentator, though Blass believes it is Sappho's.<\/div>","st":"81"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"VI. In Various Metres","st":"82"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">And thou thyself, Calliope.<\/div>","st":"82"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Hephaestion when he is analysing a metre invented by Archilochus.<\/div>","st":"82"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Sleep thou in the bosom of thy tender girlfriend.<\/div>","st":"83"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From the <em>Etymologicum Magnum<\/em>.<\/div>","st":"83"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Hither now, Muses, leaving golden ...<\/div>","st":"84"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Hephaestion as an example of a verse made of two Ithyphallics.<\/div>","st":"84"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">I have a fair daughter with a form like a golden flower, Cleïs the beloved, above whom I [prize] nor all Lydia nor lovely [Lesbos]....<\/div>","st":"85"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted and elaborately scanned by Hephaestion, although Bergk regards the lines as merely trochaic.<\/div>","st":"85"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">All joy to thee, daughter of Polyanax.<\/div>","st":"86"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Maximus Tyrius. It seems to be addressed to either Gorgo or Andromeda.<\/div>","st":"86"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"VII. In the Ionic a minore Metre","st":"87"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">In a dream I spake with the daughter of Cyprus.<\/div>","st":"87"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Hephaestion.<\/div>","st":"87"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Why, lovely swallow, daughter of Pandīon, [weary] me?<\/div>","st":"88"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Hephaestion, who says Sappho wrote whole songs in this metre. Ὦ ῎ραννα is Is. Vossius' emendation; ὠράνα is the ordinary reading, which Hesychius explains as perhaps an epithet of the swallow 'dwelling under the roof.'<\/div>","st":"88"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">She wrapped herself well in delicate hairy ...<\/div>","st":"89"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Pollux, who says the line refers to fine closely-woven linen.<\/div>","st":"89"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Sweet Mother, I cannot weave my web, broken as I am by longing for a boy, at soft Aphrodite's will.<\/div>","st":"90"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Hephaestion, as an example of metre.<\/div>","st":"90"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"VIII. Epithalamia, Bridal Songs","st":"91"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Raise high the roof-beam, carpenters. (Hymenaeus!) Like Ares comes the bridegroom, (Hymenaeus!) taller far than a tall man. (Hymenaeus!)<\/div>","st":"91"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Hephaestion as an example of a <em>mes-hymnic<\/em> poem, where the refrain follows each line.<\/div>","st":"91"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Towering, as the Lesbian singer towers among men of other lands.<\/div>","st":"92"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Demetrius, about 150 A.D.<\/div>","st":"92"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">As the sweet-apple blushes on the end of the bough, the very end of the bough, which the gatherers overlooked, nay overlooked not but could not reach.<\/div>","st":"93"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by the Scholiast on Hermogenes, and by others, to explain the word γλυκύμαλον, 'sweet-apple,' an apple grafted on a quince.<\/div>","st":"93"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">As on the hills the shepherds trample the hyacinth under foot, and the flower darkens on the ground.<\/div>","st":"94"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Demetrius, as an example of the ornament and beauty proper to a concluding sentence. Bergk first attributed the lines to Sappho.<\/div>","st":"94"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Evening, thou that bringest all that bright morning scattered; thou bringest the sheep, the goat, the child back to her mother.<\/div>","st":"95"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From the <em>Etymologicum Magnum<\/em>, where it is adduced to show the meaning of αὔως, 'dawn.' The fragment occurs also in Demetrius, as an example of Sappho's grace.<\/div>","st":"95"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">I shall be ever maiden.<\/div>","st":"96"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From a Parisian MS. edited by Cramer, adduced to show the Aeolic form of ἀεί, 'ever.'<\/div>","st":"96"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">We will give, says the father ...<\/div>","st":"97"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From a Parisian MS. edited by Cramer.<\/div>","st":"97"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">To the doorkeeper feet seven fathoms long, and sandals of five bulls' hides, the work of ten cobblers.<\/div>","st":"98"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Hephaestion, as an example of metre. Demetrius says: 'And elsewhere Sappho girds at the rustic bridegroom and the doorkeeper ready for the wedding, in prosaic rather than poetic phrase, as if she were reasoning rather than singing, using words out of harmony with dance and song.'<\/div>","st":"98"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Happy bridegroom, now is thy wedding come to thy desire, and thou hast the maiden of thy desire.<\/div>","st":"99"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Hephaestion, along with the following, to exemplify metres; both fragments seem to belong to the same ode.<\/div>","st":"99"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">And a soft [paleness] is spread over the lovely face.<\/div>","st":"100"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">In the National Library of Madrid there is a MS. of an epithalamium by Choricius, a rhetorician of Gaza, who flourished about 520 A.D., in which the lamented Ch. Graux (<em>Revue de Philologie<\/em>, 1880, p. 81) found a quotation from Sappho which is partly identical with this fragment preserved by Hephaestion.<\/div>","st":"100"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">He who is fair to look upon is [good], and he who is good will soon be fair also.<\/div>","st":"101"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Galen, the physician, writing about 160 A.D., says: 'It is better therefore, knowing that the beauty of youth is like Spring flowers, its pleasure lasting but a little while, to approve of what the Lesbian [here] says, and to believe Solon when he points out the same.'<\/div>","st":"101"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Do I still long for maidenhood?<\/div>","st":"102"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Apollonius, and by the Scholiast on Dionysius of Thrace, to illustrate the interrogative particle ἆρα, Aeolic ἦρα, and as an example of the catalectic iambic.<\/div>","st":"102"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">The bride [comes] rejoicing; let the bridegroom rejoice.<\/div>","st":"103"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Hephaestion, as a catalectic iambic.<\/div>","st":"103"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Whereunto may I well liken thee, dear bridegroom? To a soft shoot may I best liken thee.<\/div>","st":"104"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Hephaestion, as an example of metre.<\/div>","st":"104"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Hail, bride! noble bridegroom, all hail!<\/div>","st":"105"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Servius, about 390 A.D., on Vergil, <em>Georg.<\/em> i. 31; also referred to by Pollux and Julian.<\/div>","st":"105"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">For there was no other girl, O bridegroom, like her.<\/div>","st":"106"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Dionysius of Halicarnassus.<\/div>","st":"106"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Sing Hymenaeus!<br>Ah for Adonis!<\/div>","st":"107-108"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Plotius, about the fifth or sixth century A.D., to show the metre of Sappho's hymeneal odes. The text is corrupt; the first verse is thus emended by Bergk, the second by Scaliger.<\/div>","st":"107-108"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">A. Maidenhood, maidenhood, whither art thou gone away from me?<br>B. Never again will I come to thee, never again.<\/div>","st":"109"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Demetrius, who quoted the fragment to show the grace of Sappho's style and the beauty of repetition.<\/div>","st":"109"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Fool, faint not thou in thy strong heart.<\/div>","st":"110"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From a very corrupt passage in Herodian. The translation is from Bergk's former emendation— Ἄλλα μὴ κάμε τὺ στερέαν φρένα.<\/div>","st":"110"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">To himself he seems ...<\/div>","st":"111"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Apollonius, to show that the Aeolians used the digamma, ϝ. Bergk says this fragment does not belong to fr. 2.<\/div>","st":"111"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Much whiter than an egg.<\/div>","st":"112"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From Athenaeus.<\/div>","st":"112"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Neither honey nor bee for me.<\/div>","st":"113"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">A proverb quoted by many late authors, referring to those who wish for good unmixed with evil. They seem to be the words of the bride.<\/div>","st":"113"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Stir not the shingle.<\/div>","st":"114"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by the Scholiast on Apollonius Rhodius to show that χεράδες were 'little heaps of stones.'<\/div>","st":"114"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Thou burnest us.<\/div>","st":"115"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Apollonius to show the Aeolic form of ἡμᾶς, 'us.'<\/div>","st":"115"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">A napkin dripping.<\/div>","st":"116"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">From the Scholiast on Aristophanes' <em>Plutus<\/em>, quoted to show the meaning of ἡμιτύβιον, 'a half worn out shred of linen with which to wipe the hands.'<\/div>","st":"116"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">She called him her son.<\/div>","st":"117"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Apollonius to show the Aeolic use of the digamma.<\/div>","st":"117"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"IX. Epigrams","st":"118"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">All three are preserved only in the <em>Greek Anthology<\/em>. The authenticity of the last, fr. 120, is doubtful. To none of them does Bergk restore the form of the Aeolic dialect.<\/div>","st":"118"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Maidens, dumb as I am, I speak thus, if any ask, and set before your feet a tireless voice: To Leto's daughter Aethopia was I dedicated by Arista daughter of Hermocleides son of Saonaïades, thy servant, O queen of women; whom bless thou, and deign to glorify our house.<\/div>","st":"118"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">This is the dust of Timas, whom Persephone's dark chamber received, dead before her wedding; when she perished, all her fellows dressed with sharpened steel the lovely tresses of their heads.<\/div>","st":"119"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Over the fisherman Pelagon his father Meniscus set weel and oar, memorial of a luckless life.<\/div>","st":"120"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Bergk sees no reason to accept the voice of tradition in attributing this epigram to Sappho.<\/div>","st":"120"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"X. Miscellaneous","st":"121"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus says:— 'It is something natural that people who fancy themselves beautiful and elegant should be fond of flowers; on which account the companions of Persephone are represented as gathering flowers. And Sappho says she saw—'<\/div>","st":"121"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">A maiden full tender plucking flowers.<\/div>","st":"121"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Far sweeter of tone than harp, more golden than gold.<\/div>","st":"122-123"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Demetrius as an example of hyperbolic phrase. A commentator on Hermogenes the rhetorician says: 'These things basely flatter the ear, like the erotic phrases which Anacreon and Sappho use, γάλακτος λευκοτέρα <em>whiter than milk<\/em>, ὕδατος ἁπαλωτέρα <em>fresher than water<\/em>, πηκτίδων ἐμμελεστέρα <em>more musical than the harp<\/em>, ἵππου γαυροτέρα <em>more skittish than a horse<\/em>, ῥόδων ἁβροτέρα <em>more delicate than the rose<\/em>, ἱματίου ἑανοῦ μαλακωτέρα <em>softer than a fine robe<\/em>, χρυσοῦ τιμιωτέρα <em>more precious than gold<\/em>.'<\/div>","st":"122-123"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Demetrius says:—<\/div>","st":"124"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Wherefore also Sappho is eloquent and sweet when she sings of Beauty, and of Love and Spring and the Kingfisher; and every beautiful expression is woven into her poetry, besides what she herself invented.'<\/div>","st":"124"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Maximus Tyrius says:—<\/div>","st":"125"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Diotima says that Love flourishes in prosperity, but dies in adversity; a sentiment which Sappho comprehends when she calls Love γλυκίπικρος <em>bitter-sweet<\/em> [cf. fr. 40] and ἀλγεσίδωρος giver of pain. Socrates calls Love the wizard, Sappho μυθοπλόκος <em>the weaver of fictions<\/em>.'<\/div>","st":"125"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">My darling.<\/div>","st":"126"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Quoted by Julian, and by Theodoras Hyrtacenus in the twelfth century A.D., as of 'the wise Sappho.' Bergk says Sappho would have written τὸ μέλημα ὦμον in her own dialect.<\/div>","st":"126"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Aristides says:—<\/div>","st":"127"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Το γάνος <em>the brightness<\/em> standing over the whole city, οὐ διαφθεῖρον τὰς ὄψεις <em>not destroying the sight<\/em>, as Sappho says, but developing at once and crowning and watering with cheerfulness; in no way ὑακινθίνω ἄνθει ὅμοιον <em>like a hyacinth-flower<\/em>, but such as earth and sun never yet showed to men.'<\/div>","st":"127"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Pollux writes:—<\/div>","st":"128"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Anacreon ... says they are crowned also with <em>dill<\/em>, as both Sappho [cf. fr. 78] and Alcaeus say; though these also say σελίνοις <em>with parsley<\/em>.'<\/div>","st":"128"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Philostratus says:—<\/div>","st":"129"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Thus contend [the maidens] ῥοδοπήχεις καὶ ἑλικώπιδες καὶ καλλιπάρῃοι καὶ μελίφωνοι <em>with rosy arms and glancing eyes and fair cheeks and honeyed voices<\/em>—this indeed is Sappho's sweet salutation.'<\/div>","st":"129"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">And Aristaenĕtus:—<\/div>","st":"129"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Before the porch the most musical and μειλιχόφωνοι <em>soft-voiced<\/em> of the maidens sang the hymeneal song; this indeed is Sappho's sweetest utterance.'<\/div>","st":"129"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Libanius the rhetorician, about the fourth century A.D., says:—<\/div>","st":"130"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'If therefore nought prevented Sappho the Lesbian from praying νύκτα αὐτῇ γενέσθαι διπλασίαν <em>that the night might be doubled for her<\/em>, let me also ask for something similar. Time, father of year and months, stretch out this very year for us as far as may be, as, when Herakles was born, thou didst prolong the night.'<\/div>","st":"130"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Strabo says:—<\/div>","st":"131"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'a hundred furlongs further (from Elaea, a city in Aeolis) is Cané, the promontory opposite to Lectum, and forming the Gulf of Adramyttium, of which the Elaïtic Gulf is a part. Canae is a small city of the Locrians of Cynus, over against the most southerly extremity of Lesbos, situated in the Canaean territory, which extends to Arginusae and the overhanging cliff which some call <em>Aega<\/em>, as if \"a goat,\" but the second syllable should be pronounced long, Aegā, like ἀκτά and ἀρχά, for this was the name of the whole mountain which at present is called Cané or Canae ... and the promontory itself seems afterwards to have been called <em>Aega<\/em>, as Sappho says the rest Canē or Canae.'<\/div>","st":"131"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">The Scholiast on Apollonius Rhodius says:—<\/div>","st":"132"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Apollonius calls Love the son of Aphrodite, Sappho <em>of Earth and Heaven<\/em>.'<\/div>","st":"132"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">But the Argument prefixed to Theocritus, <em>Idyl<\/em> xiii., says:—<\/div>","st":"132"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Sappho called <em>Love the child of Aphrodite and Heaven<\/em>.'<\/div>","st":"132"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">And Pausanias, about 180 A.D., says:—<\/div>","st":"132"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'On Love Sappho the Lesbian sang many things which do not agree with one another.'<\/div>","st":"132"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Himerius says:—<\/div>","st":"133"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Thou art, I think, an evening-star, of all stars the fairest: this is Sappho's song to Hesperus.' And again: 'Now thou didst appear like that fairest of all stars; for the Athenians call thee Hesperus.'<\/div>","st":"133"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Bergk thinks Sappho's line ran thus:—<\/div>","st":"133"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Of all stars the fairest.<\/div>","st":"133"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">The Scholiast on Apollonius Rhodius says:—<\/div>","st":"134"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'The story of the love of Selēnē is told by Sappho, and by Nicander in the second book of his <em>Europa<\/em>; and it is said that Selene came to Endymion in the same cave' (on Mount Latmus in Caria).<\/div>","st":"134"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">The Scholiast on Hesiod, <em>Op. et D.<\/em>, 74, says:—<\/div>","st":"135"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Sappho calls Persuasion Ἀφροδίτης θυγατέρα <em>Daughter of Aphrodite<\/em>.'<\/div>","st":"135"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Maximus Tyrius says:— 'Socrates blames Xanthippe for lamenting his death, as Sappho blames her daughter—'<\/div>","st":"136"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">For lamentation may not be in a poet's house: such things befit not us.<\/div>","st":"136"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Aristotle, in his <em>Rhetoric<\/em>, ii. 23, writes:— ἢ ὥσπερ Σαπφώ, ὅτι το ἀποθνήσκειν κακόν· οἱ θεοὶ γὰρ οὕτω κεκρίκασιν· ἀπέθνησκον γὰρ ἄν.<\/div>","st":"137"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Gregory, commenting on Hermogenes, also quotes the same saying:— οἷον φησιν ἡ Σαπφώ, ὅτι τὸ ἀποθνήσκειν κακόν· οἱ θεοὶ γὰρ οὕτω κεκρίκασιν· ἀπέθνησκον γὰρ ἄν, εἴπερ ἦν καλὸν τὸ ἀποθνήσκειν.<\/div>","st":"137"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Several attempts have been made to restore these words to a metrical form, and this of Hartung's appears to be the simplest:—<\/div>","st":"137"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Death is evil; the Gods have so judged: had it been good, they would die.<\/div>","st":"137"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus says:—<\/div>","st":"138"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Naucratis has produced some celebrated courtesans of exceeding beauty; as Dōricha, who was beloved by Charaxus, brother of the beautiful Sappho, when he went to Naucratis on business, and whom she accuses in her poetry of having robbed him of much. Herodotus calls her Rhodōpis, not knowing that Rhodopis was different from the Doricha who dedicated the famous spits at Delphi.'<\/div>","st":"138"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Herodotus, about 440 B.C., said:—<\/div>","st":"138"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Rhodopis came to Egypt with Xanthes of Samos; and having come to make money, she was ransomed for a large sum by Charaxus of Mitylene, son of Scamandronymus and brother of Sappho the poetess. Thus Rhodopis was made free, and continued in Egypt, and being very lovely acquired great riches for a Rhodopis, though no way sufficient to erect such a pyramid [as Mycerīnus'] with. For as any one who wishes may to this day see the tenth of her wealth, there is no need to attribute any great wealth to her. For Rhodopis was desirous of leaving a monument to herself in Greece, and having had such a work made as no one ever yet devised and dedicated in a temple, to offer it at Delphi as a memorial of herself: having therefore made from the tenth of her wealth a great number of iron spits for roasting oxen, as far as the tenth allowed, she sent them to Delphi; and they are still piled up behind the altar which the Chians dedicated, and opposite the temple itself. The courtesans of Naucratis are generally very lovely: for in the first place this one, of whom this account is given, became so famous that all the Greeks became familiar with the name Rhodopis; and in the next place, after her another whose name was Archidĭce became celebrated throughout Greece, though less talked about than the former. As for Charaxus, after ransoming Rhodopis he returned to Mitylene, where Sappho ridiculed him bitterly in an ode.'<\/div>","st":"138"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">And Strabo:—<\/div>","st":"138"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'It is said that the tomb of the courtesan was erected by her lovers: Sappho the lyric poet calls her <em>Dōricha<\/em>. She was beloved by Sappho's brother Charaxus, who traded to the port of Naucratis with Lesbian wine. Others call her Rhodopis.'<\/div>","st":"138"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">And another writer (<em>Appendix Prov.<\/em>, iv. 51) says:—<\/div>","st":"138"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'The beautiful courtesan Rhodopis, whom Sappho and Herodotus commemorate, was of Naucratis in Egypt.'<\/div>","st":"138"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus says:—<\/div>","st":"139"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'The beautiful Sappho in several places celebrates her brother, Larĭchus, as cup-bearer to the Mitylenaeans in the town-hall.'<\/div>","st":"139"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">The Scholiast on the <em>Iliad<\/em>, xx. 234, says:—<\/div>","st":"139"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'It was the custom, as Sappho also says, for well-born and beautiful youths to pour out wine.'<\/div>","st":"139"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Palaephătus, probably an Alexandrian Greek, says:—<\/div>","st":"140"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Phaon gained his livelihood by a boat and the sea; the sea was crossed by a ferry; and no complaint was made by any one, since he was just, and only took from those who had means. He was a wonder among the Lesbians for his character. The goddess—they call Aphrodite \"the goddess\"—commends the man, and having put on the appearance of a woman now grown old, asks Phaon about sailing; he was swift to wait on her and carry her across and demand nothing. What thereupon does the goddess do? They say she transformed the man and restored him to youth and beauty. This is that Phaon, her love for whom Sappho several times made into a song.'<\/div>","st":"140"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">The story is repeated by many writers.<\/div>","st":"140"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">[Fr. 141 now appears as fr. 57A, <em>q.v.<\/em>]<\/div>","st":"141"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Pausanias says:—<\/div>","st":"142"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Yet that gold does not contract rust the Lesbian poetess is a witness, and gold itself shows it.'<\/div>","st":"142"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">And the Scholiast on Pindar, <em>Pyth.<\/em>, iv. 407:— 'But gold is indestructible; and so says Sappho,'<\/div>","st":"142"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Gold is son of Zeus, no moth nor worm devours it.<\/div>","st":"142"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Sappho's own phrase is lost.<\/div>","st":"142"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Aulus Gellius, about 160 A.D., writes:—<\/div>","st":"143"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Homer says Niobe had six sons and six daughters, Euripides seven of each, Sappho <em>nine<\/em>, Bacchylides and Pindar ten.'<\/div>","st":"143"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Cf. fr. 31, the only line extant from the ode here referred to.<\/div>","st":"143"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Servius, commenting on Vergil, <em>Aeneid<\/em>, vi. 21, says:—<\/div>","st":"144"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Some would have it believed that Theseus rescued along with himself seven boys and seven maidens, as Plato says in his <em>Phaedo<\/em>, and Sappho in her lyrics, and Bacchylides in his dithyrambics, and Euripides in his <em>Hercules<\/em>.'<\/div>","st":"144"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">No such passage from Sappho has been preserved.<\/div>","st":"144"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Servius, commenting on Vergil, <em>Eclog.<\/em>, vi. 42, says:—<\/div>","st":"145"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Prometheus, son of Iapĕtus and Clymĕne, after he had created man, is said to have ascended to heaven by help of Minerva, and having applied a small torch [or perhaps 'wand'] to the sun's wheel, he stole fire and showed it to men. The Gods being angered hereby sent two evils upon the earth, fevers and disease [the text is here obviously corrupt; it ought to be 'women and disease' or 'fevers and women'], as Sappho and Hesiod tell.'<\/div>","st":"145"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Philostratus says:—<\/div>","st":"146"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Sappho loves the Rose, and always crowns it with some praise, likening beautiful maidens to it.'<\/div>","st":"146"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Himerius says:—<\/div>","st":"147"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'These gifts of yours must now be likened to those of the leader of the Muses himself, as Sappho and Pindar, in an ode, adorn him with golden hair and lyres, and attend him with a team of swans to Helicon while he dances with Muses and Graces; or as poets inspired by the Muses crown the Bacchanal (for thus the lyre calls him, meaning Dionȳsos), when Spring has just flashed out for the first time, with Spring flowers and ivy-clusters, and lead him, now to the topmost heights of Caucasus and vales of Lydia, now to the cliffs of Parnassus and the rock of Delphi, while he leaps and gives his female followers the note for the Evian tune.'<\/div>","st":"147"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Eustathius says:—<\/div>","st":"148"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'There is, we see, a vagabond friendship, as Sappho would say, καλὸν δημόσιον, <em>a public blessing<\/em>.'<\/div>","st":"148"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">This appears to have been said against Rhodopis.<\/div>","st":"148"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">The <em>Lexicon Seguerianum<\/em> defines—<\/div>","st":"149"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Ἄκακος <em>one who has no experience of ill<\/em>, not, one who is good-natured. So Sappho uses the word.'<\/div>","st":"149"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">The <em>Etymologicum Magnum<\/em> defines—<\/div>","st":"150"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Ἀμαμαξύς <em>a vine trained on long poles<\/em>, and says Sappho makes the plural ἀμαμάξυδες. So Choeroboscus, late in the sixth century A.D., says 'the occurrence of the genitive ἀμαμαξύδος [the usual form being ἀμαμάξυος] in Sappho is strange.'<\/div>","st":"150"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">The <em>Etymologicum Magnum<\/em> says of Ἀμάρα, <em>a trench for watering meadows<\/em>, 'because it is raised by a water-bucket, ἄμη being a mason's instrument'—that it is a word Sappho seems to have used; and Orion, about the fifth century A.D., also explains the word similarly, and says Sappho used it.<\/div>","st":"151"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Apollonius says:—<\/div>","st":"152"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'And in this way metaplasms of words [<em>i.e.<\/em>, tenses or cases formed from non-existent presents or nominatives] arise, like ἐρυσάρματες [chariot-drawing], λῖτα [cloths], and in Sappho τὸ αὔα, Dawn.'<\/div>","st":"152"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">And the <em>Etymologicum Magnum<\/em> says:—<\/div>","st":"152"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'We find παρὰ τὴν αὔαν [during the morning] in Aeolic, for \"during the day.\"'<\/div>","st":"152"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">The <em>Etymologicum Magnum<\/em> says:— 'Αὔως or ἠώς, that is, the day; thus we read in Aeolic. Sappho has—'<\/div>","st":"153"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Queen Dawn.<\/div>","st":"153"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus says:—<\/div>","st":"154"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'The βάρωμος [<em>baromos<\/em>] and σάρβιτος [<em>sarbĭtos<\/em>], both of which are mentioned by Sappho and Anacreon, and the Magădis and the Triangles and the Sambūcae, are all ancient instruments.'<\/div>","st":"154"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus in another place, apparently more correctly, gives the name of the first as βάρμος [<em>barmos<\/em>].<\/div>","st":"154"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Pollux says:—<\/div>","st":"155"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Sappho used the word βεῦδος for <em>a woman's dress<\/em>, a <em>kimberĭcon<\/em>, a kind of short transparent frock.'<\/div>","st":"155"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Phrynĭchus the grammarian, about 180 A.D., says:—<\/div>","st":"156"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Sappho calls <em>a woman's dressing-case<\/em>, where she keeps her scents and such things, γρύτη.'<\/div>","st":"156"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Hesychius, about 370 A.D., says Sappho called Zeus Ἕκτωρ, <em>Hector<\/em>, <em>i.e.<\/em> 'holding fast.'<\/div>","st":"157"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">A Parisian MS. edited by Cramer says:—<\/div>","st":"158"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Among the Aeolians ζ is used for δ, as when Sappho says ζάβατον for διάβατον, <em>fordable<\/em>.'<\/div>","st":"158"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">A Scholiast on Homer quotes ἀγαγοίην, <em>may I lead<\/em>, from Sappho.<\/div>","st":"159"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Eustathius, commenting on the <em>Iliad<\/em>, quotes the grammarian Aristophanes [about 260 B.C.] saying that Sappho calls a wind that is as if twisted up and descending, a cyclone, ἄνεμον κατάρη, <em>a wind rushing from above<\/em>.<\/div>","st":"160"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Choeroboscus says:—<\/div>","st":"161"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Sappho makes the accusative of κίνδυνος <em>danger<\/em> κίνδυν.'<\/div>","st":"161"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Another writer, in the <em>Codex Marc.<\/em>, says:—<\/div>","st":"161"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Sappho makes the accusative κίνδυνα.'<\/div>","st":"161"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Joannes Alexandrinus, about the seventh century A.D., says:—<\/div>","st":"162"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'The acute accent falls either on the last syllable or the last but one or the last but two, but never on the last but three; the accent of Μήδεϊα [<em>Medeia<\/em> the sorceress, wife of Jason] in Sappho is allowed by supposing the ει to form a diphthong.'<\/div>","st":"162"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">An unknown author, in <em>Antiatticista<\/em>, says:—<\/div>","st":"163"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Sappho, in her second book, calls σμίρνα <em>myrrh<\/em> μύρρα.'<\/div>","st":"163"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">A treatise on grammar edited by Cramer says:—<\/div>","st":"164"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'The genitive plural of Μοῦσα is Μωσάων among the Laconians, Μοισάων <em>of the Muses<\/em> in Sappho.'<\/div>","st":"164"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Phrynichus says:—<\/div>","st":"165"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Νίτρον <em>natron<\/em> (carbonate of soda) is the form 'an Aeolian would use, such as Sappho, with a ν; but,' he goes on, 'an Athenian would spell it with a λ, λίτρον.'<\/div>","st":"165"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">A Scholiast on Homer, <em>Iliad<\/em>, iii. 219, says:—<\/div>","st":"166"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Sappho said πολυΐδριδι <em>of much knowledge<\/em> as the dative of πολύϊδρις.'<\/div>","st":"166"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Photius, in his <em>Lexicon<\/em>, about the ninth century A.D., says:—<\/div>","st":"167"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Θάψος is a wood with which they dye wool and hair yellow, which Sappho calls Σκυθικόν ξύλον <em>Scythian wood<\/em>.'<\/div>","st":"167"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">And the Scholiast on Theocritus, <em>Idyl<\/em> ii. 88, says:—<\/div>","st":"167"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Θάψος is a kind of wood which is also called σκυθάριον or Scythian wood, as Sappho says; and in this they dip fleeces and make them of a quince-yellow, and dye their hair yellow; among us it is called χρυσόξυλον <em>gold-wood<\/em>.'<\/div>","st":"167"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Ahrens thinks that here the Scholiast quoted Sappho, and he thus restores the verses:—<\/div>","st":"167"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Scythian wood, in which they dip fleeces and make them quince-coloured, and dye their hair yellow.<\/div>","st":"167"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">The <em>Etymologicum Magnum<\/em> says:—<\/div>","st":"168"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'The Aeolians say Τίοισιν ὀφθάλμοισιν <em>with what eyes<\/em> ... [using τίοισι for τίσι, the dative plural of τίς] as Sappho does.'<\/div>","st":"168"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Orion of Thebes, the grammarian, about 450 A.D., says:—<\/div>","st":"169"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'In Sappho χελώνη is χελύνη <em>a tortoise<\/em>'; which is better written χελύνα, or rather χέλυνα, as other writers imply.<\/div>","st":"169"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">Pollux says:—<\/div>","st":"170"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">'Bowls with a boss in the middle are called βαλανειόμφαλοι, circular-bottomed, from their shape, χρυσόμφαλοι, gold-bottomed, from the material, like Sappho's χρυσαστράγαλοι, <em>with golden ankles<\/em>.'<\/div>","st":"170"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"The Fayum Fragments","st":"A"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">Thou seemest not to care <em>to return<\/em> my favour; and <em>indeed<\/em> thou didst fly away from famous ... <em>of<\/em> the fair <em>and noble<\/em> ... to thy friends, <em>and painest me<\/em>, and castest <em>reproach<\/em> at <em>me.<\/em> Truly <em>thou mayst swell, and sate thyself<\/em> with milking a goat of Scyros. <em>For<\/em> my mood is <em>not so<\/em> soft-hearted to those soever to whom <em>it is disposed<\/em> unfriendly ... <em>nor<\/em> ....<\/div>","st":"A"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">The words which are here italicised are those which alone are extant in full in the manuscript; the others are only plausible guesses, though some of them are indicated by the existence of accents and portions of letters. In the Egyptian Museum at Berlin there are some ancient manuscripts which were bought in the summer of 1879, and which are believed to have come from Medînet-el-Fayûm in Central Egypt, near the ancient Arsinoë or Crocodilopolis. A tiny scrap of parchment among these was deciphered by Professor F. Blass of Kiel, and described by him with much minuteness in the <em>Rheinisches Museum<\/em> for 1880, vol. xxxv. pp. 287-290. The two fragments, distinguished by Blass as A. and B., occur, the one on the front, the other on the back of the scrap of parchment. They were edited by Bergk, in the fourth (posthumous) edition of his <em>Poetae Lyrici Graeci<\/em>, 1882, vol. iii. pp. 704, 705. Blass ascribed the verses to Sappho, and he is still of opinion that they are hers, from the metre, the dialect, and 'the colour of the diction,' to use his own expression in a letter to me. Bergk, however, prints them as of uncertain origin, <em>fragmenta adespota<\/em> (56 A., 56 B). A restoration of such imperfect fragments must needs be guess-work. Bergk has, however, attempted it in part, and he has accepted the emendations of Blass in lines 3-5 of fragment A.<\/div>","st":"A"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-frag\">... soul ... altogether ... I should be able ... as long indeed as to me ... to flash back ... fair face ... stained over ... friend.<\/div>","st":"B"},{"sp":"N","t":"<div class=\"sm-src\">But in the absence of any context the very meaning of the separate words is uncertain. The only additions hazarded by Bergk, or accepted by him from Blass, are given on the left of the brackets.<\/div>","st":"B"}],[]);})();